body:
## Motivation

The visual interface should be designed for the agent that consumes it, not only as a human-style screenshot transport.

A simple candidate is to overlay a lightweight coordinate mesh/grid on screenshots. The hypothesis is that a model can refer to smaller screen regions more reliably when the image exposes stable visual coordinates, especially for precise clicks, drag endpoints, small controls and recovery after an imprecise movement.

This should be treated as an experiment, not as a default UI change. A grid may help fine motor selection but can also obscure content, add visual/token cost, interfere with perception, or become unnecessary for coarse actions.

## Core hypothesis

Given the same underlying observation and task, a model-visible mesh/grid may improve spatial action selection and reduce correction loops for fine pointer operations.

Potential benefits:

- easier localization of small controls;
- more precise click/drag endpoint specification;
- easier communication between observation and action coordinates;
- fewer repeated screenshots used only to refine a point;
- a natural coarse-to-fine representation for agent motor control;
- less dependence on implicit pixel-coordinate estimation by the model.

## Do not assume the grid should always be enabled

Evaluate at least these policies:

1. **No grid** — current screenshot baseline.
2. **Always-on grid** — same deterministic overlay on every relevant screenshot.
3. **Model-selectable grid** — the planner can request a grid when useful and omit it otherwise.
4. **Coarse-to-fine grid** — coarse mesh first, then denser grid/ROI only for a selected region.

The model-selectable version may be the better interface if grids help only for detailed manipulation. The selection itself must not silently add an extra remote planner round trip; it should be possible to request the desired presentation as part of an observation/action contract where appropriate.

## Representation questions

Candidate overlay should be deterministic and machine-readable. Explore, without committing yet:

- evenly spaced vertical/horizontal lines;
- row/column labels or compact coordinate IDs;
- normalized cells independent of display resolution;
- optional local pixel or surface-relative coordinates;
- low-opacity lines that do not destroy underlying visual evidence;
- a denser ROI grid instead of making the whole frame dense;
- preserving the original unmodified frame separately from the presented overlay.

Do not make X11 root coordinates part of the semantic contract merely because the first implementation runs on Linux. The presentation should eventually map cleanly to an abstract coordinate frame / observed surface.

## Important provenance rule

The grid is a presentation transform, not the source observation.

Preserve enough identity to distinguish:

`source frame -> overlay transform -> model-visible artifact`

The original image must remain auditable/reconstructable so that a grid-related perception failure is not confused with a capture failure. This also aligns with the existing unresolved model-visible image provenance work.

## Evaluation

Run matched comparisons rather than adopting by intuition. Candidate tasks should include fine and coarse pointer actions across multiple domains, for example:

- Inkscape object selection and requested displacement;
- OpenTTD toolbar/placement endpoints;
- Calc small-cell or UI targeting;
- browser controls at different window positions/zooms;
- later Mindustry placement or other dense real-time GUI tasks.

Predeclare exact target/score before each study. Useful metrics include:

- task success / correctness hard gate;
- click target error in pixels or normalized surface units;
- drag endpoint/object displacement error;
- number of corrective observations/actions;
- planner boundaries per successful task;
- time to first correct target selection;
- model-visible image/token cost when measurable;
- failure modes caused by visual clutter or obscured content.

Use fresh window positions, zoom levels and target layouts so the model cannot simply memorize the fixture.

## Agent-centric interface principle

This experiment is part of a broader design goal:

> The Agent Interface should expose representations that are easy for an agent to perceive and control, rather than forcing the model to imitate a human visual/motor interface when a deterministic machine-oriented presentation is better.

That does **not** mean replacing the universal screenshot/keyboard/pointer fallback with app-specific APIs. The grid is an optional presentation aid over the same universal computer interface.

Future agent-centric presentation candidates can be evaluated independently (for example ROI emphasis, stable region IDs, task-relevant overlays or coordinate frames), but this issue should start with the smallest falsifiable intervention: a visual mesh/grid.

## Promotion bar

Do not promote because one demo looks easier. Retain only if the grid preserves correctness and produces a reproducible improvement on a nominated spatial/control metric across fresh cases, with its added presentation/token complexity recorded.

If always-on grids hurt general perception but model-selectable grids help fine actions, prefer the selective interface rather than treating the result as a binary grid/no-grid decision.

## Relation to current work

This directly complements the current pointer/drag work and Issue #2 architecture-risk register:

- precise pointer control currently exposes a gap between cursor path and application-level object displacement;
- bounded intermediate drag feedback is being explored;
- coordinate-frame abstraction and model-visible image provenance are already recognized risks.

A grid should be tested as an observation/presentation candidate, not used to mask unresolved pointer semantics or compensate with hard-coded per-application offsets.
